cite18†Seed Campus Recruitment L141: \nL142: cite19†Transparency L143: \nL144: cite7†Image: ByteDance Seed†lf-flow-web-cdn.doubao.com L145: \nL146: Advancing the frontier of intelligence, in service of humanity\nL147: \nL148: cite8†Image†lf-flow-web-cdn.doubao.com L149: \nL150: Join ByteDance Seed\nL151: \nL152: Copyright© 2026 Bytedance Seed\nL153: \nL154: cite20†Disclaimer L155: \nL156: Contact us : seed.feedback@bytedance.com\nL157: \nL158: cite7†Image: ByteDance Seed†lf-flow-web-cdn.doubao.com L159: \nL160: Advancing the frontier of intelligence, in service of humanity\nL161: cite8†Image†lf-flow-web-cdn.doubao.com Contact us : seed.feedback@bytedance.com\nL162: \nL163: Copyright© 2026 Bytedance Seedcite20†Disclaimer --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNew Released - Overview - Z.AI DEVELOPER DOCUMENT (https://docs.z.ai/release-notes/new-released)\nciteturn27754view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.z.ai/release-notes/new-released\",\"lineno\":55}); Total lines: 165\nL0: > ## Documentation Index\nL1: >\nL2: > Fetch the complete documentation index at: cite0†/llms.txt L3: >\nL4: > Use this file to discover all available pages before exploring further.\nL5: \nL6: cite1†Skip to main content L7: \nL8: cite2†Overview - Z.AI DEVELOPER DOCUMENT home page†z.ai L9: \nL10: Search...\nL11: \nL12: Navigation\nL13: \nL14: New Released\nL15: \nL16: cite3†Guides cite4†API Reference cite5†Coding Plan cite6†Released Notes cite7†Terms and Policy cite8†Help Center L17: \nL18: # New Released\nL19: \nL20: Follow along with updates across Z.AI’s models\nL21: \nL22: Copy page Copy page\nL23: ##\nL24: \nL25: cite9†​ L26: \nL27: Models\nL28: \nL29: cite10†​ L30: \nL31: [Button: 2026-08-26]\nL32: \nL33: GLM-5.3-Flash\nL34:   * Native visual capabilities enable the model to observe interfaces, rendering results, and interaction feedback—creating a closed loop across code, browsers, and GUIs.\nL35:   * Efficient hybrid architecture: Combines linear and sparse attention with 320B total parameters and 18B activated, significantly reducing compute and KV-cache requirements.\nL36:   * Beyond coding: Supports office document and financial research workflows, autonomously breaking down goals, using tools, and refining outputs. Learn more in our cite11†documentation .*\nL37: cite12†​ L38: \nL39: [Button: 2026-08-18]\nL40: \nL41: GLM-5.3\nL42:   * Stronger Coding Capabilities: GLM-5.3 delivers a significant improvement in coding capabilities, achieving a 50% gain over GLM-5.2 on Z.ai Code Bench and reaching state-of-the-art (SOTA) performance among open-source models on public benchmarks, including Terminal Bench 3.0.\nL43:   * Emergent Cybersecurity Capabilities: GLM-5.3 matches Mythos 5 in white-box code review and vulnerability discovery. In collaboration with multiple cybersecurity teams, it has been tested on real-world targets and has identified a total of 2,436 vulnerabilities, including 1,097 medium- and high-severity vulnerabilities.Learn more in our cite13†documentation .*\nL44: cite14†​ L45: \nL46: [Button: 2026-06-16]\nL47: \nL48: GLM-5.2\nL49:   * Supports 1M lossless context, significantly improving long-horizon task capabilities and reducing context drift and goal forgetting in complex tasks\nL50:   * Achieves open-source SOTA performance on coding and long-horizon task benchmarks, delivering more stable results in complex system engineering and deep debugging\nL51:   * Significantly improves the real-world developer experience, with more reliable project-level context handling, adherence to engineering standards, and multi-platform development. Learn more in our cite15†documentation .*\nL52: cite16†​ L53: \nL54: [Button: 2026-04-07]\nL55: \nL56: GLM-5.1\nL57:   * Designed for long-horizon tasks, GLM-5.1 can work independently for up to 8 hours in a single run, enabling a full loop from planning and execution to iterative refinement and final delivery.\nL58:   * It demonstrates stronger engineering intelligence across autonomous planning, sustained execution, bug fixing, and strategy iteration, while achieving comprehensive capability alignment with Claude Opus 4.6. Built with multi-turn SFT, RL, and a process-quality evaluation framework, GLM-5.1 further improves stability, consistency, and tool use over extended tasks. Learn more in our cite17†documentation .*\nL59: cite18†​ L60: \nL61: [Button: 2026-02-12]\nL62: \nL63: GLM-5\nL64:   * Designed for complex system engineering and long-range Agent tasks, GLM-5 shifts the paradigm from coding to engineering, demonstrating strong deep-reasoning performance in backend architecture, complex algorithms, and stubborn bug fixing.\nL65:   * It directly benchmarks against Claude Opus 4.5 in code-logic density and systems-engineering capability, and integrates DeepSeek Sparse Attention for higher token efficiency while preserving long-context quality.Learn more in our cite19†documentation .*\nL66: \nL67: cite20†​ L68: [Button: 2026-02-03]\nL69: \nL70: GLM-OCR\nL71:   * We’ve launched GLM-OCR, a compact and high-performance optical character recognition model powered by the self-developed CogViT and GLM-0.5B encoder-decoder architecture, enabling efficient cross-modal alignment through its dedicated connection layer.\nL72:   * The update leverages CLIP pre-training on billions of image-text pairs to deliver robust visual semantic understanding and key token extraction capabilities, while maintaining a lightweight design for fast inference. Learn more in our cite21†documentation .*\nL73: cite22†​ L74: \nL75: [Button: 2026-01-19]\nL76: \nL77: GLM-4.7-Flash\nL78:   * We’ve launched GLM-4.7-Flash, a lightweight and efficient model designed as the free-tier version of GLM-4.7, delivering strong performance across coding, reasoning, and generative tasks with low latency and high throughput.\nL79:   * The update brings competitive coding capabilities at its scale, offering best-in-class general abilities in writing, translation, long-form content, role play, and aesthetic outputs for high-frequency and real-time use cases. Learn more in our cite23†documentation .*\nL80: \nL81: cite24†​ L82: [Button: 2026-01-14]\nL83: \nL84: GLM-Image\nL85:   * We’ve launched GLM-Image, a state-of-the-art image generation model built on a multimodal architecture and fully trained on domestic chips, combining autoregressive semantic understanding with diffusion-based decoding to deliver high-quality, controllable visual generation.\nL86:   * The update significantly enhances performance in knowledge-intensive scenarios, with more stable and accurate text rendering inside images, making GLM-Image especially well suited for commercial design, educational illustrations, and content-rich visual applications.Learn more in our cite25†documentation .*\nL87: cite26†​ L88: \nL89: [Button: 2025-12-22]\nL90: \nL91: GLM-4.7\nL92:   * We’ve released GLM-4.7, our foundation model with significant improvements in coding, reasoning, and agentic capabilities. It delivers more reliable code generation, stronger long-context understanding, and improved end-to-end task execution across real-world development workflows.\nL93:   * The update brings open-source SOTA performance on major coding and reasoning benchmarks, enhanced agentic coding for goal-driven, multi-step tasks, and improved front-end and document generation quality. Learn more in our cite23†documentation .*\nL94: cite27†​ L95: \nL96: [Button: 2025-12-11]\nL97: \nL98: AutoGLM-Phone-Multilingual\nL99:   * We’ve launched AutoGLM-Phone-Multilingual, our latest multimodal mobile automation framework that understands screen content and executes real actions through ADB. It enables natural-language task execution across 50+ mainstream apps, delivering true end-to-end mobile control.\nL100:   * The update introduces multilingual support (English & Chinese), enhanced workflow planning capabilities, and improved task execution reliability. Learn more in our cite28†documentation .*\nL101: \nL102: cite29†​ L103: \nL104: [Button: 2025-12-10]\nL105: GLM-ASR-2512\nL106: \nL107:   * We’ve launched GLM-ASR-2512, our ASR model, delivering industry-leading accuracy with a Character Error Rate of just 0.0717, and significantly improved performance across real-world multilingual and accent-rich scenarios.\nL108:   * The update introduces enhanced custom dictionary support and expanded specialized terminology recognition. Learn more in our cite30†documentation .*\nL109: \nL110: cite31†​ L111: \nL112: [Button: 2025-12-08]\nL113: \nL114: GLM-4.6V\nL115:   * We’re excited to introduce GLM-4.6V, Z.ai’s latest iteration in multimodal large language models. This version enhances vision understanding, achieving state-of-the-art performance in tasks involving images and text.\nL116:   * The update also expands the context window to 128K, enabling more efficient processing of long inputs and complex multimodal tasks. Learn more in our cite32†documentation .*\nL117: \nL118: cite33†​ L119: \nL120: [Button: 2025-09-30]\nL121: \nL122: GLM-4.6\nL123:   * We’ve launched GLM-4.6, the flagship coding model, showcasing enhanced performance in both public benchmarks and real-world programming tasks, making it the leading coding model in China.\nL124:   * The update also expands the context window to 200K, improving its ability to handle longer code and complex agent tasks. Learn more in our cite34†documentation .*\nL125: \nL126: cite35†​ L127: \nL128: [Button: 2025-08-11]\nL129: \nL130: GLM-4.5V\nL131:   * We’ve launched GLM-4.5V, a 100B-scale open-source vision reasoning model, supporting a broad range of visual tasks including video understanding, visual grounding, GUI agents and etc.\nL132:   * The update also adds a new thinking mode. Learn more in our cite36†documentation .*\nL133: \nL134: cite37†​ L135: \nL136: [Button: 2025-08-08]\nL137: \nL138: GLM Slide/Poster Agent(beta)\nL139:   * We’ve launched GLM Slide/Poster Agent, an AI-powered creation agent that combines information retrieval, content structuring, and visual layout design to generate professional-grade slides and posters from natural language instructions.\nL140:   * The update also brings a seamless integration of content generation with design conventions. Learn more in our cite38†documentation .*\nL141: \nL142: cite39†​ L143: \nL144: [Button: 2025-07-28]\nL145: \nL146: GLM-4.5 Series\nL147:   * We’ve launched GLM-4.5, our latest native agentic LLM, delivering doubled parameter efficiency and strong reasoning, coding, and agentic capabilities.\nL148:   * It also offers seamless one-click compatibility with the Claude Code framework. Learn more in our cite40†documentation .*\nL149: \nL150: cite41†​ L151: \nL152: [Button: 2025-07-15]\nL153: \nL154: CogVideoX-3\nL155:   * We’ve launched CogVideoX-3, an incremental upgrade to our video generation model with improved quality and new features.\nL156:   * It adds support for start and end frame synthesis. Learn more in our cite42†documentation .*\nL157: \nL158: Was this page helpful?\nL159: \nL160: Yes No\nL161: \nL162: cite43†x†x.com cite44†github†github.com cite45†discord†discord.gg cite46†linkedin†www.linkedin.com L163: \nL164: cite47†Powered byThis documentation is built and hosted on Mintlify, a developer documentation platform†www.mintlify.com --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2405.18754] GIST: Greedy Independent Set Thresholding for Max-Min Diversification with Submodular Utility (https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.18754)\nciteturn27754view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn27752view1\",\"id\":37}); Total lines: 163\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Data Structures and Algorithms\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 29 May 2024 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 20 Oct 2025 (this version, v3)]\nL9: # Title:GIST: Greedy Independent Set Thresholding for Max-Min Diversification with Submodular Utility\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Matthew Fahrbach , cite7†Srikumar Ramalingam , cite8†Morteza Zadimoghaddam , cite9†Sara Ahmadian , cite10†Gui Citovsky , cite11†Giulia DeSalvo L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled GIST: Greedy Independent Set Thresholding for Max-Min Diversification with Submodular Utility, by Matthew Fahrbach and 5 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite12†View PDF cite13†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:This work studies a novel subset selection problem called max-min diversification with monotone submodular utility ($\\textsf{MDMS}$), which has a wide range of applications in machine learning, e.g., data sampling and feature selection.\nL17: Given a set of points in a metric space, the goal of $\\textsf{MDMS}$ is to maximize $f(S) = g(S) + \\lambda \\cdot \\texttt{div}(S)$ subject to a cardinality constraint $|S| \\le k$, where $g(S)$ is a monotone submodular function and $\\texttt{div}(S) = \\min_{u,v \\in S : u \\ne v} \\text{dist}(u,v)$ is the max-min diversity objective.\nL18: We propose the $\\texttt{GIST}$ algorithm, which gives a $\\frac{1}{2}$-approximation guarantee for $\\textsf{MDMS}$ by approximating a series of maximum independent set problems with a bicriteria greedy algorithm. We also prove that it is NP-hard to approximate within a factor of $0.5584$. Finally, we show in our empirical study that $\\texttt{GIST}$ outperforms state-of-the-art benchmarks for a single-shot data sampling task on ImageNet.\nL19: Comments:  | 21 pages, 3 figures\nL20: Subjects:  | Data Structures and Algorithms (cs.DS); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite14†arXiv:2405.18754 [cs.DS]\nL22:    | (or cite15†arXiv:2405.18754v3 [cs.DS] for this version)\nL23:    | cite16†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2405.18754†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: Journal reference:  | Proceedings of the 39th Annual Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2025)\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Matthew Fahrbach [cite17†view email ]\nL28: cite5†[v1] Wed, 29 May 2024 04:39:24 UTC (730 KB)\nL29: cite18†[v2] Mon, 10 Feb 2025 21:17:29 UTC (1,888 KB)\nL30: [v3] Mon, 20 Oct 2025 13:56:47 UTC (1,894 KB)\nL31: \nL32: Full-text links:\nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled GIST: Greedy Independent Set Thresholding for Max-Min Diversification with Submodular Utility, by Matthew Fahrbach and 5 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite12†View PDF L38:   * cite13†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite19†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite20†view license†creativecommons.org L42: \nL43: ### Current browse context:\nL44: \nL45: cs.DS\nL46: \nL47: cite21†< prev |   cite22†next > L48: \nL49: cite23†new | cite24†recent | cite25†2024-05 L50: \nL51: Change to browse by:\nL52: \nL53: cite26†cs L54: cite27†cs.LG L55: ### References & Citations\nL56: \nL57:   * cite28†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L58:   * cite29†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L59:   * cite30†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L60: \nL61: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL62: \nL63: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL64: \nL65: [Button: ×]\nL66: \nL67: loading...\nL68: \nL69: Data provided by:\nL70: \nL71: ### Bookmark\nL72: \nL73: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL74: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL75: \nL76: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL77: \nL78: Bibliographic Explorer (cite31†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL79: \nL80: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL81: \nL82: Connecte